{"kind":"request","text":"codex: move the step-5 succession obligation out of AGENTS.md prose and into `gs merge` itself, so a merge retires the pointers it supersedes and publishes the successors in one operation, instead of asking each agent to do it by hand afterward.\n\nWHY, measured at depth 4651 (log spans 2026-08-06 to 2026-08-12; 180 merges on main). 890 supersede acts against 812 artifact statements: the retirement bookkeeping now outnumbers the pointers it maintains. 119 of 251 live artifacts and 114 of the 121 approvals ever granted are stale, so the flare no longer discriminates — the exact failure AGENTS.md warns about (\"a flare carrying no information teaches people to ignore the flares that do\"). 23 real paths carry two or more live artifacts despite the doctrine. Supersessions split claude 537 / codex 346 / hugh 2, and the fold's author-or-ratifier rule means the merging agent often cannot retire the other agent's predecessor at all — the direct cause of the 2026-08-12 red main. A deterministic procedure (AGENTS.md's own words: \"keeping them apart is what makes the choice deterministic\") enforced by prose across every agent's context window is a tooling gap, and the project should build using its own discipline.\n\nDESIGN INTENT, with latitude on mechanics:\n- `gs merge` computes the changed paths from the merge diff (git diff --name-only merge^1 merge), retires every live artifact covered by them, and publishes the successor artifact per surviving covered path, in the same batch against one verified frontier.\n- The fold admits a merge-driven supersession regardless of the predecessor's author: the ratified approval chain that authorizes the merge also authorizes withdrawing the pointers the merge supersedes. The author-or-ratifier rule stays unchanged for free-standing supersessions.\n- The path rules stop being prose and become code pinned by tests: wider path wins when a directory and something inside it both carry live artifacts; a rename retires the old path and publishes a first artifact at the new one; a deletion retires with no successor; `.` and comma-joined pseudo-paths are refused as artifact paths at admission.\n\nThis composes with proposal eae77d2c (merge staleness evaluated as of the approval): that removes the race where retirement breaks in-flight verdicts; this removes the manual retirement itself. Either can land first.","body":{"conditions":"A merge performed with gs merge leaves no covered predecessor live and exactly one live artifact per surviving covered path, proven by fold tests that include a cross-author predecessor case. Free-standing gs supersede keeps its current author-or-ratifier authority rule, with a test. Artifact paths `.` and comma-joined strings are refused at admission, with tests. docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in the same head if the kernel-application contract changes. AGENTS.md is not rewritten under this request; that work is filed separately and rests on this one.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}